From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 13:22:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] netd: route a bypassable VPN's UIDs before the implicit
 network rules

LineageOS's firewall gives the default network an allowlist of UID
ranges (all UIDs unless its policy denies some) at the lowest
sub-priority that still makes the network the default of those UIDs.
Netd turns it into an implicit network rule for every UID at
RULE_PRIORITY_UID_IMPLICIT_NETWORK plus that sub-priority (22998), and
connect() marks a socket with the default network's netId even when a
bypassable VPN applies to the UID. Such a socket matched that rule
before the VPN's UID range rule at 24000 and left over the underlying
network, so a VPN application that allows bypass carried no traffic
from the applications it covers, while the resolver and unmarked
sockets still entered the VPN.

The UID range rule and the system permission rule of a bypassable VPN
that does not exclude local routes move to 21900, just before the
implicit network rules. AOSP needs no such order: its connect() marks
the socket with the VPN's netId, which those rules do not match. The
UID range rule still requires a socket that is neither explicitly
bound to a network nor protected from the VPN, so bound and protected
sockets bypass the VPN as before. A secure VPN's rules (13000) do not
move, and a destination that the VPN's routing table has no route to
continues to the network rules, so split tunnels keep their
fallthrough. The rules of a VPN that excludes local routes stay behind
the local routes rules.

The tests follow. The bypassable VPN cases expect the physical
network's netId in the mark, as connect() sets it, and the packet on
the VPN. A new test installs the firewall's allowlist and checks that
a covered UID's packets enter the VPN, that an uncovered UID's and a
destination outside the VPN's routes stay on the default network, and
that an explicit selection of the default network bypasses the VPN.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 server/RouteController.cpp |  6 ++++
 server/RouteController.h   | 15 +++++++++-
 tests/binder_test.cpp      | 59 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 3 files changed, 72 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/server/RouteController.cpp b/server/RouteController.cpp
index 2f0e8e2225bd49533cf2c516754375d94b11cb87..4b867113d75b355cf9b2420660ed8e26b604ef6c 100644
--- a/server/RouteController.cpp
+++ b/server/RouteController.cpp
@@ -515,6 +515,12 @@ int modifyIncomingPacketMark(unsigned netId, const char* interface, Permission p
 // Notice that this rule doesn't use the netId. I.e., no matter what netId the user's socket may
 // have, if they are subject to this VPN, their traffic has to go through it. Allows the traffic to
 // bypass the VPN if the protectedFromVpn bit is set.
+//
+// A bypassable VPN's rule leaves out sockets that selected a network explicitly. connect() marks a
+// socket with the physical default network's netId even when a bypassable VPN applies to the UID,
+// so the rule of a VPN that does not exclude local routes is placed before the implicit network
+// rules (see RULE_PRIORITY_BYPASSABLE_VPN_NO_LOCAL_EXCLUSION). A destination that the VPN's table
+// has no route to continues to those rules.
 [[nodiscard]] static int modifyVpnUidRangeRule(uint32_t table, uid_t uidStart, uid_t uidEnd,
                                                int32_t subPriority, bool secure, bool add,
                                                bool excludeLocalRoutes) {
diff --git a/server/RouteController.h b/server/RouteController.h
index d8393e75b7b788bf1086943320148ead45231028..8635895b4c2b89f12c549df908d64512e21889a5 100644
--- a/server/RouteController.h
+++ b/server/RouteController.h
@@ -48,11 +48,18 @@ constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_EXPLICIT_NETWORK                  = 16000;
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_OUTPUT_INTERFACE                  = 17000;
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_LOCAL_NETWORK                     = 20000;
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_TETHERING                         = 21000;
+// The UID range rule and the system permission rule of a bypassable VPN that does not exclude local
+// routes come before the implicit network rules below. The framework gives the default network an
+// implicit rule for every UID, and a socket that connect() marked with that network's netId would
+// match it before the VPN's rule and never enter the VPN. Explicitly selected and protected sockets
+// still skip the VPN (its UID range rule requires neither bit), and a destination that the VPN's
+// table has no route to continues to the network rules. A VPN has only sub-priority 0, so this
+// priority precedes every implicit network rule.
+constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_BYPASSABLE_VPN_NO_LOCAL_EXCLUSION = 21900;
 // Implicit rules for sockets that connected on a given network because the network was the default
 // network for the UID.
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_UID_IMPLICIT_NETWORK              = 22000;
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_IMPLICIT_NETWORK                  = 23000;
-constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_BYPASSABLE_VPN_NO_LOCAL_EXCLUSION = 24000;
 // Sets of rules used for excluding local routes from the VPN. Look up tables
 // that contain directly-connected local routes taken from the default network.
 // The first set is used for apps that have a per-UID default network. The rule
@@ -61,6 +68,7 @@ constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_BYPASSABLE_VPN_NO_LOCAL_EXCLUSION = 24000;
 // is the system default network network.
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_UID_LOCAL_ROUTES                  = 25000;
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_LOCAL_ROUTES                      = 26000;
+// A bypassable VPN that excludes local routes stays behind the local routes rules above.
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_BYPASSABLE_VPN_LOCAL_EXCLUSION    = 27000;
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_VPN_FALLTHROUGH                   = 28000;
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_UID_DEFAULT_NETWORK               = 29000;
@@ -83,6 +91,11 @@ constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_DEFAULT_NETWORK                   = 31000;
 constexpr int32_t RULE_PRIORITY_UNREACHABLE                       = 32000;
 // clang-format on
 
+static_assert(RULE_PRIORITY_TETHERING < RULE_PRIORITY_BYPASSABLE_VPN_NO_LOCAL_EXCLUSION &&
+                      RULE_PRIORITY_BYPASSABLE_VPN_NO_LOCAL_EXCLUSION <
+                              RULE_PRIORITY_UID_IMPLICIT_NETWORK,
+              "a bypassable VPN's rules must come before the implicit network rules");
+
 class UidRanges;
 
 class RouteController {
diff --git a/tests/binder_test.cpp b/tests/binder_test.cpp
index 00e6aadaf2fb54cc27854414339ec4e1da858edb..581bf62edddaa9f4dc7939239a0a010585e64199 100644
--- a/tests/binder_test.cpp
+++ b/tests/binder_test.cpp
@@ -4512,14 +4512,14 @@ TEST_P(VpnParameterizedTest, ImplicitlySelectNetwork) {
 
     // uid is neither in app range, nor in VPN range. Traffic goes through system default network.
     expectPacketSentOnNetId(AID_ROOT, SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID, systemDefaultFd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
-    // uid is in VPN range, not in app range. Traffic goes through VPN.
-    expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID3, (isSecureVPN ? SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID : VPN_NETID), vpnFd,
-                            IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
+    // uid is in VPN range, not in app range. Traffic goes through VPN. A connecting socket carries
+    // the physical network's netId even when a bypassable VPN applies to the uid, so the VPN's rule
+    // has to come before the physical network's implicit rules.
+    expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID3, SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID, vpnFd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
     // uid is in app range, not in VPN range. Traffic goes through per-app default network.
     expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID1, APP_DEFAULT_NETID, appDefaultFd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
     // uid is in both app and VPN range. Traffic goes through VPN.
-    expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID2, (isSecureVPN ? APP_DEFAULT_NETID : VPN_NETID), vpnFd,
-                            IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
+    expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID2, APP_DEFAULT_NETID, vpnFd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
 }
 
 class VpnAndSelectNetworkParameterizedTest
@@ -5188,8 +5188,9 @@ TEST_F(NetdBinderTest, UidRangeSubPriority_ImplicitlySelectNetwork) {
     int appDefault_1_Fd = sTun2.getFdForTesting();
     int vpnFd = sTun3.getFdForTesting();
     int appDefault_2_Fd = sTun4.getFdForTesting();
-    // Verify routings.
-    expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID1, VPN_NETID, vpnFd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
+    // Verify routings. The socket carries the netId of the per-app default network even though the
+    // bypassable VPN has the highest priority for TEST_UID1; the VPN's rule takes the traffic.
+    expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID1, APP_DEFAULT_1_NETID, vpnFd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
     expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID2, APP_DEFAULT_1_NETID, appDefault_1_Fd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
     expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID3, APP_DEFAULT_2_NETID, appDefault_2_Fd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
     expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID4, APP_DEFAULT_2_NETID, appDefault_2_Fd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
@@ -5206,6 +5207,50 @@ TEST_F(NetdBinderTest, UidRangeSubPriority_ImplicitlySelectNetwork) {
     }
 }
 
+// LineageOS's firewall gives the default network an allowlist: the UID ranges that its policy
+// allows, all UIDs unless some are denied, at the lowest sub-priority that still makes the network
+// the default of those UIDs. Its implicit network rule must not take the traffic of a UID that a
+// bypassable VPN applies to, although connect() marks the socket with the default network's netId.
+//
+// SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID uses sTun (default route), VPN_NETID uses sTun3 (route 2001:db8::/32 only).
+TEST_F(NetdBinderTest, BypassableVpnBeatsDefaultNetworkAllowlist) {
+    createVpnAndOtherPhysicalNetwork(SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID, APP_DEFAULT_NETID, VPN_NETID,
+                                     false /* secure */);
+    constexpr int ALL_UIDS_END = 2147483647;
+    EXPECT_TRUE(mNetd->networkAddUidRangesParcel(
+                            makeNativeUidRangeConfig(SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID,
+                                                     {makeUidRangeParcel(0, ALL_UIDS_END)},
+                                                     UidRanges::SUB_PRIORITY_LOWEST))
+                        .isOk());
+    EXPECT_TRUE(mNetd->networkAddUidRanges(VPN_NETID, {makeUidRangeParcel(TEST_UID1)}).isOk());
+
+    int systemDefaultFd = sTun.getFdForTesting();
+    int vpnFd = sTun3.getFdForTesting();
+
+    // The VPN applies to TEST_UID1: its connected and unconnected sockets go into the VPN.
+    expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID1, SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID, vpnFd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
+    expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID1, NETID_UNSET, vpnFd, UNCONNECTED_SOCKET);
+    // The VPN does not apply to TEST_UID2: it stays on the default network.
+    expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID2, SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID, systemDefaultFd, IMPLICITLY_SELECT);
+    expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID2, NETID_UNSET, systemDefaultFd, UNCONNECTED_SOCKET);
+
+    // A destination that the VPN has no route to continues to the default network.
+    const in6_addr outsideVpnAddr = {
+            {// 2607:f0d0:1002::4
+             .u6_addr8 = {0x26, 0x07, 0xf0, 0xd0, 0x10, 0x02, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 4}}};
+    Fwmark fwmark;
+    EXPECT_TRUE(sendIPv6PacketFromUid(TEST_UID1, outsideVpnAddr, &fwmark, systemDefaultFd));
+    EXPECT_EQ(SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID, static_cast<int>(fwmark.netId));
+
+    // A socket that selects the default network explicitly bypasses the VPN.
+    {
+        ScopedUidChange scopedUidChange(TEST_UID1);
+        EXPECT_EQ(0, setNetworkForProcess(SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID));
+        expectPacketSentOnNetId(TEST_UID1, SYSTEM_DEFAULT_NETID, systemDefaultFd,
+                                EXPLICITLY_SELECT);
+    }
+}
+
 class PerAppNetworkPermissionsTest : public NetdBinderTest {
   public:
     int bindSocketToNetwork(int sock, int netId, bool explicitlySelected) {
